      "text": "To solve the problem, we start by noting that when the square \\(ABCD\\) with side length 1 unit is folded along the lines \\(DE\\) and \\(DF\\), the sides \\(AD\\) and \\(CD\\) coincide and lie on the diagonal \\(BD\\). This means that the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are positioned such that the fold of the square is along the line that extends from \\(D\\) to the midpoint of \\(BD\\).\n\nLet \\(AE = CF = x\\). Since \\(AB = 1\\), it follows that \\(EB = 1 - x\\) and \\(FC = 1 - x\\).\n\nWhen the square is folded, the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) essentially \"trap\" themselves at the midpoint of \\(BD\\) on \\(AD\\) and \\(CD\\), respectively. This implies that the distance \\(DE\\) and \\(DF\\) are equal to the distance from \\(D\\) to the midpoint of \\(BD\\).\n\nSince \\(DE\\) and \\(DF\\) are equal to the distance from \\(D\\) to the midpoint of \\(BD\\), and this distance is also the perpendicular distance from \\(D\\) to the intersection of the folds, which is the perpendicular bisector of \\(BD\\), we can use similar triangles to find the value of \\(x\\).\n\nBy symmetry and the fact that the fold line is along the diagonal \\(BD\\), we can use the Pythagorean theorem in the right triangle formed by the fold. Specifically, the segment \\(DE\\) and \\(DF\\) are both equal to the same value, and we can relate this to the side length of the square.\n\nThe fold line divides the square into two equal parts, and the length of \\(DE\\) and \\(DF\\) can be found using the fact that the fold creates a right triangle with the side of the square as the hypotenuse and the segments \\(AE\\) and \\(CF\\) as the legs. The length of \\(AE\\) and \\(CF\\) can be derived from the Pythagorean theorem in a smaller right triangle formed by the fold.\n\nSince the fold line is along the diagonal and divides the square symmetrically, we can use the fact that the length of \\(AE\\) and \\(CF\\) is related to the height of the smaller right triangle formed by the fold.\n\nBy solving the equation derived from the similar triangles and the Pythagorean theorem, we find that \\(x = \\sqrt{2} - 1\\). Therefore, \\(k = 2\\) and \\(m = 1\\), and the integer value of \\(k + m\\) is \\(2 + 1 = 3\\).\n\nThus, the integer value of \\(k + m\\) is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).",
